le" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/c3d512c9e89079d86c29d1aaa8feb9fa8a47345fda4a1c0e2f2f5228246d0ef5.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $2$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+2).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(t+2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "917e7f34b46182f09dcb2df1b7ef2a85593c8d08684973866d7c2ebca0fa9584"} {"question" "q-331246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2u(t+2)"] ["math" "2u(t-2)"] ["math" "u(t+2)"] ["math" "u(t+2)+1"] ["math" "u(t-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "u(t+2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/bcf4076849dd96858c586f8696414789b3f68074b87652601245407142f2b8fc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we scale $y=u(t)$ by a factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(t).$\n\n- There is no horizontal shift or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "917e7f34b46182f09dcb2df1b7ef2a85593c8d08684973866d7c2ebca0fa9584"} {"question" "q-331249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2u(t)"] ["math" "2u(t)"] ["math" "2u(t)+1"] ["math" "2u(t-1)"] ["math" "u(t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answe