"answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-u(-t)+2"] ["math" "2u(-t)"] ["math" "u(-t)+2"] ["math" "u(-t)-2"] ["math" "u(t)+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-352909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "u(-t)+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/c3680f88195487cb34a0c288dc605bbd731868433f52317a7cd31797caad3dd2.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/a1748c6e23d99ad6ef43d5aceedcadb7a12fcdbef5064f93202d2fe5ca3d98d7.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-1))=u(1-t).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(1-t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "917e7f34b46182f09dcb2df1b7ef2a85593c8d08684973866d7c2ebca0fa9584"} {"question" "q-331267" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331267" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2u(1-t)"] ["math" "u(-1-t)"] ["math" "u(1-t)"] ["math" "u(1-t)+1"] ["math" "u(t-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-331267" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "u(1-t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-352913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/fe876b6075fa3e292595976c6d0077fec9c43b0af6a13c165abbd754fa7fcbe7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-352913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/385fe662259d5c933171b68f6b4b7227051921c3327da8f1ca10ff3bd6c5da3f.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y=u(-(t+1))=u(-t-1).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=4u(-t-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=4u(-t-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-352913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "917e7f34b46182f09dcb2df1b7ef2a85593c8d08684973866d7c2ebca0fa9584"} {"question" "q-352913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-352913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4u(-t-1)"] ["math" "4u(1-t)"] ["math" "4u(t+1)"] ["math" "4u(t-1)"] ["math" "u(-t-4)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "q-352913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4u(-t-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8dde3b02dae44d30dfb9f925f4e8b66b746f9ec011754fa1b21266d4b9288e3"} {"question" "e-21591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $f(t) = 9u(t-9)$ at $t=9,$ where $u$ is the unit step function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/example-21591.json" "file_sha256" "bd8cff97d5b0e1b5662ff830898fc25dd10b1097a05ffd25022de8066570bb36"} {"question" "e-21591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(t)$ at $t=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9) & =9u(9-9) \\\\  & =9u(0) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(9)=9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/example-21591.json" "file_sha256" "bd8cff97d5b0e1b5662ff830898fc25dd10b1097a05ffd25022de8066570bb36"} {"question" "e-21592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/fe9901de89c17bccdaa8c0f95440a079e6537dc42fe858fd836d89eb62f29ca7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/example-21592.json" "file_sha256" "aa69296a22587379b3cef1dbce3851ec9207f1d7c523eaa85938adf243e3d770"} {"question" "e-21592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $2$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+2).$\n\n- Then, we scale $y=u(t+2)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=3u(t+2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=3u(t+2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/example-21592.json" "file_sha256" "aa69296a22587379b3cef1dbce3851ec9207f1d7c523eaa85938adf243e3d770"} {"question" "e-21594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/74bc7efebfaa6a798b848d9f0bbc400bc84a727a933fef32069fec6a87e6ed6b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/example-21594.json" "file_sha256" "b7130d7eb8e943275c4e59e1bd45690e3b595f40f61495e5127d05bed8e7038e"} {"question" "e-21594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y = u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y = u(t)$ to the given function $y = f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y = u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y = u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y = u(-(t+1)) = u(-1 - t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y = 2u(-1 - t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t) = 2u(-1 - t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/example-21594.json" "file_sha256" "b7130d7eb8e943275c4e59e1bd45690e3b595f40f61495e5127d05bed8e7038e"}] "usage" {"q-331270" [] "e-21591" [] "e-21594" [] "e-21592" [] "q-331249" []} "basis" 1403 "retractions" [[17592186152479 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2760/Source/Images/q-331249.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t)$, where $u$ denotes the unit step function?"] [17592186152481 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2760/Source/Images/q-331270.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t)$, where $u$ denotes the unit step function?"] [17592186151827 :question/problem "Evaluate $f(t) = 9u(t-9)$ at $t=9$, where $u$ is the unit step function."] [17592186151827 :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{1, t \\ge 0,; 0, t < 0\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(t)$ at $t=9$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(9) &= 9u(9 - 9) \\\\\n&= 9u(0) \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(9)=9\n$$"] [17592186151828 :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t)$, where $u$ denotes the unit step function?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2760/Source/Images/b25aad85ae1374bd6c1bd86713ad24ca.png)"] [17592186151828 :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{1, t \\ge 0,; 0, t < 0\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2760/Source/Images/cea5e5cfbce8e6708425a867337b8411.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t)$, we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $2$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+2)$.\n- Then, we scale $y=u(t+2)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=3u(t+2)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(t)=3u(t+2)\n$$"] [17592186151829 :question/problem "The graph of $y = f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t)$, where $u$ denotes the unit step function?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2760/Source/Images/6058d85fd3a0bb42589d8c84a6f4f946.png)"] [17592186151829 :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{1, t \\ge 0,; 0, t < 0\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y = u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2760/Source/Images/63ce6d8bac7002ace80730a3619b1370.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y = u(t)$ to the given function $y = f(t)$, we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y = u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y = u(-t)$.\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y = u(-(t+1)) = u(-1 - t)$.\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y = 2u(-1 - t)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2u(-1 - t)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186149715 17592186149716 17592186149717 17592186149718 17592186149719 17592186149727 17592186149728 17592186149729 17592186149730 17592186149731] "immutable_fields" [[17592186149714 :answer-field/correct] [17592186149714 :answer-field/id] [17592186149714 :answer-field/key] [17592186149714 :answer-field/type] [17592186149726 :answer-field/correct] [17592186149726 :answer-field/id] [17592186149726 :answer-field/key] [17592186149726 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
